[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Italian Game"]
[Variation "Giuoco Piano: Pianissimo, Main Line"]
[ECO "C54"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "d4-break,slow-build"]
[For "white"]
[Kind "main"]
[Opponent "3...Bc5"]

1. e4 { Takes the centre and opens lines for the queen and the bishop on f1. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 { Develops a piece and attacks the e5 pawn, so Black must defend it. } 2... Nc6 { Defends e5 with a piece and develops. Black never plays 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 (the Stafford Gambit) or 3...Nd4 (the Blackburne Shilling): traps for beginners that lose against anyone who knows the answer. } 3. Bc4 { Points the bishop at f7, the weakest square in Black's camp: at the start only the king defends it. [%cal Gc4f7] } 3... Bc5 { Copies White: the bishop eyes f2 and Black can castle soon. It also avoids 3...Nf6 4.Ng5, which asks a beginner for several exact moves. } 4. c3 { Prepares d4. With a pawn on c3, White can take the centre and hit the bishop on c5 in one move. } 4... Nf6 { Develops with an attack on e4, so White has to defend the pawn. } ( 4... d6 5. d3 { The same quiet set-up as the main line, reached by a different move order. } 5... Nf6 ) ( 4... Qe7 5. O-O { Gets the king safe first. With the queen on e7 there is no hurry for d3. } 5... Nf6 ) 5. d3 { Guards the pawn on e4 and keeps the centre closed. White builds slowly first; the d4 break can wait until the pieces are ready. } 5... d6 { Gives e5 a pawn to defend it and frees the c8 bishop. } ( 5... a6 6. O-O d6 ) ( 5... O-O 6. O-O d6 ) 6. O-O { Gets the king safe before the centre opens. } 6... O-O { Castle now: the king is safe behind its pawns and the rook is ready for the centre. } ( 6... a6 7. Re1 ) ( 6... Bg4 7. h3 { Asks the pinning bishop to decide: take on f3 or retreat. Either way the pin on the f3 knight goes. } 7... Bh5 8. Nbd2 ) 7. Re1 { Defends the pawn on e4 a second time, so the knight on f3 is free to move, and puts the rook on the file where the game will open. } 7... a6 { Makes room on a7, so the bishop can step back there if White plays d4. It also stops Bb5 or Nb5 ideas. } ( 7... Ng4 8. Re2 { The rook guards f2 from the side, so the bishop on c5 and the knight on g4 are aiming at nothing. } ) ( 7... b5 8. Bxb5 { The b-pawn has no defender, so the bishop takes it and hits the knight on c6 as well. } 8... Ne7 9. d4 ) 8. Bb3 { Steps back out of reach of ...b5 and keeps the bishop on the diagonal that points at f7. } 8... Ba7 { Keeps the bishop on the long diagonal, out of reach of a pawn on d4, where it still presses on f2. } 9. h3 { Stops ...Bg4, which would pin the knight on f3 to the queen. } 9... h6 { Stops Bg5, which would pin the knight on f6 to the queen. } 10. Nbd2 { Brings out the last minor piece; it heads for f1 and then g3, the usual route in this set-up. } 10... Re8 11. Nf1 Be6 12. Ng3 { The knight reaches g3, eyeing f5 and h5. Plan: keep the bishop on b3, add Be3, and push d4 when Black cannot punish it. If Black leaves the book, castle, Re1, h3 and Nbd2 are safe. } *
